    },
    missing: {
      type: 'array',
      description: 'items the investigation should have included, each with file:line',
      items: { type: 'string' },
    },
    verdict: { type: 'string', enum: ['sound', 'sound-with-corrections', 'unreliable'] },
  },
  required: ['checked', 'wrong', 'missing', 'verdict'],
}

const INVESTIGATIONS = [
  {
    key: 'w4',
    schema: AUDIT_SCHEMA,
    prompt: `${COMMON}

TASK (W4, redacted provider output). Inventory every path by which native tool or provider output (stdout/stderr of cargo, npm, twine, git, gh, curl, and HTTP bodies) reaches: (a) error values and their Display, (b) live stderr/stdout logging by the CLI command runner, (c) JSON output and reports, (d) GitHub workflow summaries and step outputs written by the scripts under .github/actions and .github/workflows, and (e) durable files (release intent, receipt, decision, observations). Start from: crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release/provider/*.rs, crates/callisto-graph/src/error.rs (CommandFailure and its variants), crates/callisto-cli/src/runner.rs, crates/callisto-model/src/registry.rs (redact_known_secrets, redact_command_stderr), crates/callisto-model/src/exec.rs, crates/callisto-model/src/release_observation.rs (ProviderIndeterminateCause), and the action scripts. For each path say whether arbitrary provider text can reach the sink (free_text) and what redaction is applied. Then list provider failures that currently collapse into one closed cause (for example whether an authentication failure is distinguishable from a transport failure or a malformed response, and whether any of them could be read as "remote object absent"). Then propose the minimal closed causes to add, naming where each would be classified. State which of these outputs are persisted to a durable file today: verify whether a receipt is written only when every operation succeeded and whether failed-observation details are persisted anywhere. There is no function named redact_native_output; do not cite it.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'wire',
    schema: WIRE_SCHEMA,
    prompt: `${COMMON}

TASK (D01 wire-compatibility matrix). For each persisted or exchanged release document - ReleaseDecisionV1, ReleaseInputSnapshotV1, ReleaseIntentV1, ArtifactManifestV1, ReleaseRunEnvelopeV1, ReleaseReceiptV1 (see crates/callisto-model/src/release*.rs and crates/callisto-model/src/release/) - establish: the version the writer emits; every version the deserializer accepts; what digest or hash binds it and whether nested documents' versions are validated against it; what changed semantically between accepted versions; what happens to an older or newer document (accepted, migrated or rejected) with the exact error code or diagnostic; and every reader and writer (CLI commands such as release plan, release execute, release artifact-manifest and release-pr decide/verify; the committed .callisto/release-decision.json; jq or shell in .github/actions and .github/workflows). Also establish what a rerun does with an intent written by an older version (look at release plan/execute and the staleness checks in crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release/capability.rs and release_execution.rs), and list gaps where a reader has no version gate. Include the JSON schema guard tests (crates/callisto-cli/tests/schema_guard_test.rs) as evidence of what is pinned. Do not propose a universal rule; produce the per-document facts.`,
  },
]

phase('Investigate')

const W1_PROMPT = `You are implementing one small CI change in the Callisto repository. Work only inside your own git worktree (you were started in one). Do NOT commit, push, or open a PR; leave the change uncommitted and report the absolute path of your worktree and its branch name in your final message.

GOAL: add a job to .github/workflows/callisto-ci.yml that proves CI's own read-only GITHUB_TOKEN can download one asset of the latest published release and verify its attestation with exactly the flags the installer uses. Reference: .github/actions/setup-callisto/scripts/install-callisto.sh (the verify_asset function: gh attestation verify "$file" --repo orin-dx/callisto --signer-workflow orin-dx/callisto/.github/workflows/callisto-release.yml --deny-self-hosted-runners).

REQUIREMENTS
- Job id and display name of your choosing, e.g. "Release Asset Attestation". permissions: contents: read only (add attestations: read only if verification demonstrably needs it). No registry secrets, no repository secrets, no checkout of untrusted code needed unless a script from the repo is used.
- Trigger scope: it runs on pushes to main and on pull_request events whose head repository is this repository. Use an if: expression for that. Add a one-line YAML comment saying fork PRs do receive a read-only GITHUB_TOKEN and only lack secrets, and that same-repo-only is a cost/policy choice. Do not claim fork PRs get no token.
- Steps (gh is preinstalled on GitHub's ubuntu runners; do not add a third-party action unless pinned by full SHA like the rest of the file; prefer plain run steps): select the latest release, download exactly one named asset (callisto-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz) with gh release download --repo "$GITHUB_REPOSITORY" --pattern ... into $RUNNER_TEMP, then run the verifier with the three flags above. Use env: GH_TOKEN: \${{ github.token }}. Never interpolate \${{ }} expressions directly into the run: script body; pass values through env.
- Negative control inside the job: copy the downloaded asset, append one byte, and assert that the same verifier command FAILS on the copy (so the check cannot pass vacuously). Make the failure message explicit.
- No-release behavior: if the repository has no published release yet, the job must succeed with a ::notice:: saying so, and not fail. Implement it by checking gh release list output, not by ignoring errors.
- set -euo pipefail in each run step. Keep comments to one short line each, only where the why is not obvious.

VALIDATION (do all you can; report exactly which you ran and which you could not run, never infer a pass)
- Look at how the rest of .github/workflows/callisto-ci.yml and the justfile validate workflows (just zizmor, just workflow-contracts, .github/tests/verify-action-pins.sh, actionlint with -ignore=\\$/.+). Also grep the repo (.github/tests/*.sh, justfile, docs, any list of required check names or job names) for places that enumerate CI jobs, and update them consistently if they must know about the new job.
- Run the validators that are installed (actionlint, zizmor, the contract scripts) against your change and quote their real output/exit codes.
- You may exercise the download+verify logic locally against the real release if gh is authenticated: use GH_CONFIG_DIR pointing at an empty directory and GH_TOKEN="$(gh auth token)" to mimic CI. Do not print the token.

Final message: worktree path, branch, the full diff of every file changed, validators run with exit codes, validators not run and why, and any place you were unsure.`

const [chains, w1] = await Promise.all([
  pipeline(
    INVESTIGATIONS,
    (item) => agent(item.prompt, { label: `investigate:${item.key}`, phase: 'Investigate', schema: item.schema }),
    (result, item) => {
      if (!result) return null
      return agent(
        `${COMMON}

You are an independent skeptic. Another agent produced the JSON below for this task:

${item.prompt}

Its result:
${JSON.stringify(result, null, 1)}

Re-open every file:line it cites and check the claim against the actual code. List each claim that is wrong or unsupported, with what the code actually says and where. Then search the same area for relevant items it MISSED (a sink, a document, a reader, a failure mode) and list them with file:line. Default to marking a claim wrong if you cannot find it at the cited location. Return verdict 'sound' only if no claim is wrong and nothing material is missing.`,
        { label: `verify:${item.key}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERIFY_SCHEMA }
      ).then((verdict) => ({ key: item.key, result, verdict }))
    }
  ),
  agent(W1_PROMPT, { label: 'w1-ci-job', phase: 'W1 lane', isolation: 'worktree' }),
])

const out = {}
for (const c of chains.filter(Boolean)) out[c.key] = { result: c.result, verdict: c.verdict }
log(`investigations completed: ${Object.keys(out).join(', ') || 'none'}; w1 lane ${w1 ? 'returned' : 'failed'}`)
return { investigations: out, w1 }

